1 NAME Ida Tobiasdtr. Røiseland /Tobiasdtr./
2 GIVN Ida Tobiasdtr. Røiseland
2 SURN Tobiasdtr.
2 _MARNM Chamness
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 21 AUG 1867
2 ADDR
3 CITY Røiseland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1952
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ida R. Chamness was born at Roiseland, County of Kvinnesdal, Norway on 8/21/1867, the daugher of Tobias Tollaksen Røiseland. Her parents were Friends, members of the Norse Yearly Meeting. A Friend from Iowa visited
2 CONC  that yearly meeting in about 1879, and Ida's two older brothers went to America with him. Another brother came to America, and then in 1887, Ida and her sister Theoline came to Le Grand, Iowa, accompained by their father, who ret
2 CONC urned to Norway.  In the course of a religious visit, Benjamin Pickett visited Friends meeting at Stavanger, with a young man named Lester Chamness as companion. Stavanger was the meeting Ida attended, and this meeting with Lester
2 CONC  led up to their marriage in a meeting at Stavanger on 9/23/1890. He took his bride back to his home near Emporia, Kansas. In 1894, they visited Norway with their little son, Merlin. Ida was recommended as a minister by Cottonwood
2 CONC  Monthly Meeting (Kansas) on 3/9/1895. In 1899, she was liberated to attende New England and Canada Yearly Meetings. Lester bought a place near Stavanger, Iowa, in 1900, but they did not move there until the spring of 1901. In 190
2 CONC 7, they bought a farm near West Branch, Iowa, and this became Ida's home for the rest of her life. Ida's health was frail, but this did not prevent her from travelling extensiverly in the ministry. She visited New England Yearly M
2 CONC eeting in 1901, 1902, 1907 and 1913; Canada Yearly Meeting in  1905, 1907, 1910 and 1913; Philidelphia Yearly Meeting in 1903, 1907 and 1909; Ohio Yearly Meeting in 1911, and that in Kansas in 1915. When at Philidalphia Yearly Mee
2 CONC ting in 1907, she met Mary Stokes, who became her lifelong friend, assisting her financially and accompanying her on many of her religious journeys.  Ida Chamness was very active in the ministry in North Carolina during the time o
2 CONC f the separation there (1904-1911). In 1904 she visited both quarters (Eastern and Southern). Benjamin Brown, a North Carolina minister, was very kind in assisting her in getting about, which was very difficult because of her poor
2 CONC  health. She was there again in 1906, 1908, 1910, 1911 and 1913. Her ministry seems to have been well received everywhere she went, and it would appear she played no small part in the establishment of the Conservative Yearly Meeti
2 CONC ng in North Carolina. In 1905-06, Ida visited Norway, attending the Norse Yearly Meeting. During this visit, he mother died. She went again in 1909, bringing her father back with her for a visit. In 1912, accompanied by Mary Stoke
2 CONC s and Joel Johnson of Windsor, Virginia, a minister of North Carolina Yearly Meeting, she visited Norway, England, Wales and Scotland. In 1914 she was again in Norway.  Perhaps no one traveled more extensively in the ministry duri
2 CONC ng this period than Ida Chamness. However, she had enemies in her home meeting, who frequently prevented her from getting the customary minutes of liberation. In such cases, she went without them. She felt strongly that God called
2 CONC  her to go, and did not feel it righte to let man stand in her way. This trouble culminated in Ida's disownment by West Branch Monthly Meeting in second month, 1917. Her husband left her almost immediately after this event, going 
2 CONC to Alabama, and did not return until shortly before his death, which occurred on 7/4/1939. Ida had been accused of mistreating her husband. Perhaps the fact that he came back to her to die may shed a little light on the facts of t
2 CONC he case.  It is distressing to go into some of the things that Ida had to hear, but it seems due to her memory not to pass them by without some mention. She appealed her disownment, but it was upheld by the yearly meeting in 1919.
2 CONC  Between the time of her disownment by the monthly meeting and this final action, some of the members of the meeting at West Branch filed a complaint of insanity against her. The hearing was held at Tipton (Iowa) on 1/30/1919 and 
2 CONC her enemies were ignominlously defeated.  Ida Chamness did not wither way after her disownment and desertion by her husband. She was a firm believer in the principles of Friends without any deviation. From the time of her disownme
2 CONC nt until her death, she held meeting in her own home. She brought her children up in the old Friends belief, including plainness of speech and dress. It seems remarkable that Friends principles and practices were standfestly maint
2 CONC ained by many American yearly meetings until about this time, with such a rapid departure since, to the great loss to the Church.  In 1927, Ida visited in the ministry England, Scotland, Norway, Germany and Finland. In 1930, she a
2 CONC ttended Ohio and North Carolina Yearly Meetings. She was ever ready to help the unfortunate and to offer such a refuge when possible. When one John Mercer, age 28, was arraigned at Tipton (Iowa) in seventh month 1936 for a hold-up
2 CONC  murder committed in 1930, Ida felt to attend, and knelt in prayer in the courtroom. The newspaper reported that the "prisoner was affected". She visited him in the jail after this hearing and several times later at Fort Madison, 
2 CONC accompanied by some of her daughters. About three months before his execution, he joined the Chamness meeting, and as far as appears, was truly converted. After his execution on 1/24/1938, the Chamness claimed the body, which was 
2 CONC interred on their farm after the funeral in their home. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Var i norge sommeren 1938. Datteren Aina og Jenny var og med. Aina var syk og ble på St. Josefs hospital i Kongensgate i Kristiansand. Vannsyste i magen. I denne forbindelse kom det bil til Røiseland for første gang. Far fikk 10 e
2 CONC ller 20 cent fordi han var så snill å bar inn vann og ved til dem hver morgen hele sommeren. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ida Chamness lived on a farm near West Branch, Iowa, and was very active in the Society of Friends. (http://beta.worldcat.org/archivegrid/data/148137022)
